WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:02.635
-Our next guest is
a best-selling author

00:00:02.635 --> 00:00:04.637
and a contributing opinion
writer for "The New York Times."

00:00:04.637 --> 00:00:07.907
His book, "The Beauty Of Dusk:
On Vision Lost and Found,"

00:00:07.907 --> 00:00:09.208
is on sale now.

00:00:09.208 --> 00:00:11.277
Please welcome back to the show,
Frank Bruni, everyone!

00:00:11.277 --> 00:00:13.880
♪♪
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:20.153 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:20.153 --> 00:00:26.426 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:26.426 --> 00:00:28.161
Welcome back!
-Thank you.

00:00:28.161 --> 00:00:30.129
I feel a little self-conscious.
-How so?

00:00:30.129 --> 00:00:32.432
I don't have any eggs
or farm products of any kind.

00:00:32.432 --> 00:00:34.133
-Oh.
-I'm sorry.

00:00:34.133 --> 00:00:36.536
-Pretty bad form, Frank.
-I know, I know.

00:00:36.536 --> 00:00:38.138
If you have me back,
I'll bring a whole cow.

00:00:38.138 --> 00:00:40.773
-Alright. Well, you know, 'cause
you could have also brought me

00:00:40.773 --> 00:00:42.575
something from North Carolina.

00:00:42.575 --> 00:00:44.377
You are now living
in North Carolina.

00:00:44.377 --> 00:00:46.845
-I am.
-And, you know, like anybody who

00:00:46.845 --> 00:00:48.681
writes for "The New York Times"
as long as you have,

00:00:48.681 --> 00:00:51.985
I associate you as a New Yorker,
and I imagine a lot of

00:00:51.985 --> 00:00:54.387
your friends who are New Yorkers
do too.

00:00:54.387 --> 00:00:57.289
Do they ask you all the time how
you're liking North Carolina?

00:00:57.289 --> 00:00:58.858
-They ask me
if I've lost my mind.

00:00:58.858 --> 00:01:00.793
-Yeah.
-When you leave New York,

00:01:00.793 --> 00:01:03.863
people feel like you must be
exiled or punished. Right?

00:01:03.863 --> 00:01:06.866
But I mean, I have woods
near my house, I have a creek.

00:01:06.866 --> 00:01:09.402
My dog now chases deer
every day.

00:01:09.402 --> 00:01:12.271
In Central Park, she was chasing
smaller, different critters.

00:01:12.271 --> 00:01:14.373
-Wow.
-That weren't as attractive.

00:01:14.373 --> 00:01:15.841
There are upsides
to leaving New York.

00:01:15.841 --> 00:01:18.644
-There are upsides.
You're teaching at Duke.

00:01:18.644 --> 00:01:20.880
You've written a lot about
higher education over the years.

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:22.414
How are you finding life in it?

00:01:22.414 --> 00:01:23.850
-Humbling.
-Uh-huh.

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:25.317
-I mean, teaching, we know this,

00:01:25.317 --> 00:01:27.186
but I think we too often forget
it in this country.

00:01:27.186 --> 00:01:29.589
Teaching is really important
work, and it's really hard work.

00:01:29.589 --> 00:01:32.591
And I think I'm getting better
at it every week,

00:01:32.591 --> 00:01:35.862
but I'm humbled by
what a responsibility it is.

00:01:35.862 --> 00:01:37.330
-Yeah, I would imagine.

00:01:37.330 --> 00:01:40.766
And you did find time to write
your second memoir.

00:01:40.766 --> 00:01:42.468
-Yeah. Don't say it that way.

00:01:42.468 --> 00:01:45.705
-Well, it's just a weird night,
'cause, you know,

00:01:45.705 --> 00:01:48.440
Harvey was out here,
and Harvey's, you know, older,

00:01:48.440 --> 00:01:50.043
I think he would admit to that,
than you are.

00:01:50.043 --> 00:01:51.511
And yet he just wrote his first.

00:01:51.511 --> 00:01:53.546
And here you are being like,
"I've already had two in me."

00:01:53.546 --> 00:01:56.282
-I know, and his life is
much, much more interesting.

00:01:56.282 --> 00:01:58.484
When people say
"your second memoir,"

00:01:58.484 --> 00:02:00.587
it's like they're saying,
"Who do you think you are?"

00:02:00.587 --> 00:02:02.254
[ Laughter ]

00:02:02.254 --> 00:02:04.491
But in my defense,
since my first memoir,

00:02:04.491 --> 00:02:06.492
I had something
really crazy happen.

00:02:06.492 --> 00:02:08.027
-Yes.
-And so this is about that.

00:02:08.027 --> 00:02:09.896
-Right, because
your first memoir

00:02:09.896 --> 00:02:10.964
you were here talking about,

00:02:10.964 --> 00:02:12.231
was about your relationship
to food

00:02:12.231 --> 00:02:14.333
and writing about food
and loving food.

00:02:14.333 --> 00:02:18.004
And then you had something
very traumatic happen in 2017.

00:02:18.004 --> 00:02:21.407
You woke up, and the vision
in one of your eyes was gone.

00:02:21.407 --> 00:02:23.843
-I woke up one morning,
and there was a fog

00:02:23.843 --> 00:02:25.678
over the right half
of my field of vision.

00:02:25.678 --> 00:02:27.780
And at first, I thought,
"Okay, this is gonna be okay

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:29.348
in a couple of hours.
I'm a boomer.

00:02:29.348 --> 00:02:31.451
Everything is fixable,
medicine --"

00:02:31.451 --> 00:02:33.152
I figured, "I'll go to
the doctor tomorrow.

00:02:33.152 --> 00:02:35.621
I'll get a pill, or they'll tell
me to do eye calisthenics,

00:02:35.621 --> 00:02:37.556
you know, and then everything
will be okay."

00:02:37.556 --> 00:02:39.525
But it wasn't okay.
And in a matter of days,

00:02:39.525 --> 00:02:41.927
I was told that I'd had
a kind of stroke,

00:02:41.927 --> 00:02:44.130
that the vision in my right eye
was gone forever,

00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:47.232
and then, most terrifyingly,
that I was initially told

00:02:47.232 --> 00:02:50.036
there was a 40% chance
it would happen in my left eye

00:02:50.036 --> 00:02:51.637
and I'd go blind.

00:02:51.637 --> 00:02:53.239
I've since been told,
"No, that's wrong.

00:02:53.239 --> 00:02:54.373
It's more like 20%."

00:02:54.373 --> 00:02:56.709
But a 20% chance of
losing your vision,

00:02:56.709 --> 00:02:58.110
those stakes are big enough

00:02:58.110 --> 00:03:00.179
that 20% doesn't feel
that comforting.

00:03:00.179 --> 00:03:01.413
You know?
-I would imagine.

00:03:01.413 --> 00:03:03.316
And which is very --

00:03:03.316 --> 00:03:06.018
why, of course, that explains
why you write a second book.

00:03:06.018 --> 00:03:07.519
It's not really a memoir.

00:03:07.519 --> 00:03:09.155
It's about this moment of time
you're going through right now.

00:03:09.155 --> 00:03:10.857
And I'm wondering,
when you approached it,

00:03:10.857 --> 00:03:15.227
was writing about it
part of a way of processing

00:03:15.227 --> 00:03:16.862
what you were going through?
-100%.

00:03:16.862 --> 00:03:19.565
Yeah, I mean, I feel
really lucky because in being

00:03:19.565 --> 00:03:22.301
a journalist and in having
the tools of going out

00:03:22.301 --> 00:03:25.037
to report and learn information
and talk to people in the book,

00:03:25.037 --> 00:03:27.706
we've been saying it's a memoir,
but much of the book,

00:03:27.706 --> 00:03:29.508
more of the book is really
me profiling people

00:03:29.508 --> 00:03:31.778
who've been through really
difficult struggles,

00:03:31.778 --> 00:03:34.080
especially before old age,
as a sort of foretaste

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:36.315
of old age, and how they've
dealt with those.

00:03:36.315 --> 00:03:39.752
So, in the book, I'm more
student than teacher,

00:03:39.752 --> 00:03:42.855
and it was really helpful
to take that journey,

00:03:42.855 --> 00:03:44.423
I mean in terms of processing
everything.

00:03:44.423 --> 00:03:47.426
-And, you know, there is
this thread of optimism

00:03:47.426 --> 00:03:49.161
throughout the book
and the people you talk to

00:03:49.161 --> 00:03:51.764
and the stories of overcoming
what you said,

00:03:51.764 --> 00:03:53.533
the sort of hurdles
we don't expect

00:03:53.533 --> 00:03:55.701
at this point in our life.
We would expect older.

00:03:55.701 --> 00:03:59.138
You talked to a judge,
who was pretty moving.

00:03:59.138 --> 00:04:00.907
-The judge I talked to said
something to me that

00:04:00.907 --> 00:04:02.875
just really sticks with me
and I think is central

00:04:02.875 --> 00:04:04.377
to the book's message,
which is, he said,

00:04:04.377 --> 00:04:06.378
"Starfish can regrow limbs,

00:04:06.378 --> 00:04:08.914
but that's nothing compared to
what people can do."

00:04:08.914 --> 00:04:11.217
And that's what I found
as I talked to people,

00:04:11.217 --> 00:04:12.685
and that's what I found
in myself.

00:04:12.685 --> 00:04:16.088
If you had told me five years
ago, "You're gonna wake up

00:04:16.088 --> 00:04:17.790
with half of your vision gone,
and you're gonna be told

00:04:17.790 --> 00:04:20.493
you might go blind," I would
tell you that I'm gonna end up

00:04:20.493 --> 00:04:22.161
in the fetal position
for about a year.

00:04:22.161 --> 00:04:23.429
-Sure.
-Or forever.

00:04:23.429 --> 00:04:25.398
-Yeah.
-And that's not what happened,

00:04:25.398 --> 00:04:27.733
and that's not because I'm
the least bit extraordinary.

00:04:27.733 --> 00:04:29.401
It's because I'm like
everybody else.

00:04:29.401 --> 00:04:32.471
We are stronger than we know, we
are more resilient than we know,

00:04:32.471 --> 00:04:35.875
and that's what I learned
and what I want people to learn

00:04:35.875 --> 00:04:37.443
about themselves
or trust about themselves,

00:04:37.443 --> 00:04:40.680
especially as they age
and the chances of some sort of

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:42.048
physical thing happening
increase.

00:04:42.048 --> 00:04:44.583
-That's an incredible gift
to give people,

00:04:44.583 --> 00:04:46.085
and I'm so appreciative.

00:04:46.085 --> 00:04:48.755
But, of course, you also got
a great reward by writing it.

00:04:48.755 --> 00:04:51.123
You found out you had a fan

00:04:51.123 --> 00:04:53.792
that is a pretty cool fan
to have when you write a book.

00:04:53.792 --> 00:04:55.928
-Oh, yes.
-Yeah.

00:04:55.928 --> 00:04:57.930
-Yeah, last week,
I was interviewed

00:04:57.930 --> 00:05:00.099
on her website by Oprah Winfrey.

00:05:00.099 --> 00:05:01.167
-By "the" Oprah.

00:05:01.167 --> 00:05:04.002
-By Oprah, yeah.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:04.002 --> 00:05:05.805
-And you'd never spoken to her.

00:05:05.805 --> 00:05:07.974
-I had spoken to Oprah twice
as a reporter

00:05:07.974 --> 00:05:10.109
when I was profiling people
whom she knew

00:05:10.109 --> 00:05:12.911
and she would -- Each time,
I arranged a phone interview

00:05:12.911 --> 00:05:14.380
and the phone rang, because she
was going to give me

00:05:14.380 --> 00:05:16.682
five minutes about the person
I was profiling.

00:05:16.682 --> 00:05:18.851
And each time, when you picked
up the phone and she said,

00:05:18.851 --> 00:05:21.787
"It's Oprah,"
it's like it's God.

00:05:21.787 --> 00:05:23.122
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:23.122 --> 00:05:24.924
-Oprah doesn't exist as a voice.

00:05:24.924 --> 00:05:27.259
Well, I mean, this time,
I'm there in my living room,

00:05:27.259 --> 00:05:30.162
Zooming with her,
and she comes on and she says,

00:05:30.162 --> 00:05:31.430
"Frank Bruni doesn't know this,

00:05:31.430 --> 00:05:33.231
but he's one of my favorite
people in the world,

00:05:33.231 --> 00:05:35.401
and I can't count the number of
times I've wanted to

00:05:35.401 --> 00:05:36.969
pick up the phone and call him."

00:05:36.969 --> 00:05:39.038
And I'm thinking like,
"Oprah, the line wasn't busy.

00:05:39.038 --> 00:05:41.607
You could have called at
any moment, and you still can.

00:05:41.607 --> 00:05:42.875
Like, I'm here," you know?

00:05:42.875 --> 00:05:45.778
-It's very funny to me
that Oprah thought

00:05:45.778 --> 00:05:47.879
she couldn't get your number.

00:05:47.879 --> 00:05:49.081
-Well, she told me --

00:05:49.081 --> 00:05:50.516
I've known Gayle King
for a number of years.

00:05:50.516 --> 00:05:51.884
Terrific, terrific person.

00:05:51.884 --> 00:05:53.986
And she told me, "I'm always
telling Gayle how jealous

00:05:53.986 --> 00:05:55.821
I am that you two
text and call."

00:05:55.821 --> 00:05:58.190
And again, I'm thinking,
"Oprah, we can do it, too.

00:05:58.190 --> 00:06:00.626
It's like a done deal.
It's fine."

00:06:00.626 --> 00:06:06.032
-Has this process and the loss
of half your vision,

00:06:06.032 --> 00:06:08.767
has it changed the way you
write, changed the way you read?

00:06:08.767 --> 00:06:09.968
-It's made everything harder.

00:06:09.968 --> 00:06:11.870
I read much more slowly
than I used to.

00:06:11.870 --> 00:06:14.974
I write much more slowly because
there's something about the soup

00:06:14.974 --> 00:06:17.109
of my vision that makes a gumbo
of my thoughts.

00:06:17.109 --> 00:06:18.744
-Sure, that's fascinating.

00:06:18.744 --> 00:06:21.013
-Yeah, no, I'll write for half
an hour and thinking I've done

00:06:21.013 --> 00:06:22.915
really great work,
and I'll go back and look at it,

00:06:22.915 --> 00:06:26.052
and not only are there all these
typos, but it is not as good

00:06:26.052 --> 00:06:27.519
on first draft as it used to be.

00:06:27.519 --> 00:06:30.522
So I budget extra time
and I just adjust accordingly.

00:06:30.522 --> 00:06:32.892
I mean, I'm lucky.
I still get to write.

00:06:32.892 --> 00:06:34.093
People still read my words.

00:06:34.093 --> 00:06:35.528
You're talking to me
about my book.

00:06:35.528 --> 00:06:38.064
I choose to focus on that.
-Well, let's all focus on that.

00:06:38.064 --> 00:06:39.865
And thank you so much
for making the time.

00:06:39.865 --> 00:06:41.266
It is always so great
to see you, Frank.

00:06:41.266 --> 00:06:42.568
-Thank you.
-Really appreciate it.

00:06:42.568 --> 00:06:43.936
Frank Bruni, everybody.

00:06:43.936 --> 00:06:46.072
"The Beauty of Dusk"
is available now.

